WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.920
Think about the last time you needed
an exchange rate or a shipment

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.053
update for a report.

00:00:05.093 --> 00:00:08.993
You probably opened a website,
found the number, and copied it into

00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:12.706
Excel. It's a small task, but it
adds up when you are doing it every

00:00:12.746 --> 00:00:17.123
week. In her BrightLeaf example, Sarah
checks the dollar against Chinese

00:00:17.163 --> 00:00:20.899
yuan and Mexican peso before
approving import orders.

00:00:20.939 --> 00:00:25.174
She also checks the weather in
Shenzhen, where two of her suppliers are

00:00:25.214 --> 00:00:28.577
based. Over a month, she
does 31 of these checks.

00:00:28.617 --> 00:00:32.608
At 4 or 5 minutes each, that's
more than 2 hours spent looking up

00:00:32.798 --> 00:00:34.378
information and copying it across.

00:00:34.607 --> 00:00:38.387
An API gives us a way to get the
information directly from the system

00:00:38.427 --> 00:00:42.658
that holds it. That's how Stocky
will connect to Odoo and check things

00:00:42.698 --> 00:00:47.105
outside the ERP. Today, you will
try two APIs yourself, straight from

00:00:47.145 --> 00:00:50.901
your browser. You won't need to install
anything, sign in, or write code

00:00:50.941 --> 00:00:52.033
for these examples.

00:00:52.073 --> 00:00:55.521
We will start with a restaurant
example to explain how it works.

00:00:55.561 --> 00:00:59.197
Then we will check the weather in
Shenzhen and ask for exchange rates

00:00:59.237 --> 00:01:01.343
for the yuan, peso and dirham.

00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:05.014
I will show you how to read the
responses, even if the format looks

00:01:05.064 --> 00:01:05.969
unfamiliar at first.

00:01:06.009 --> 00:01:09.692
We will also look at asking for
information versus submitting something,

00:01:09.732 --> 00:01:13.187
the numbers that tell you whether
a request worked, and API keys.

00:01:13.227 --> 00:01:14.707
Let's start with the restaurant.

00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:17.573
Let's start with a picture.

00:01:17.613 --> 00:01:19.193
Imagine you walk into a restaurant.

00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:22.633
You're hungry, you know what you
want, but you don't walk into the

00:01:22.673 --> 00:01:26.533
kitchen. You don't open the fridge,
you don't touch the chef's knives,

00:01:26.573 --> 00:01:28.633
and you definitely don't cook.

00:01:28.673 --> 00:01:31.573
Why? Because the kitchen isn't yours.

00:01:31.613 --> 00:01:35.813
It's busy, it's organised in its own
way, and if every customer walked

00:01:35.853 --> 00:01:37.289
in, it would be chaos.

00:01:37.608 --> 00:01:39.648
So instead, you talk to the waiter.

00:01:39.688 --> 00:01:43.388
You say, "One chicken burrito,
please, not too spicy."

00:01:43.428 --> 00:01:46.968
The waiter takes your order to the
kitchen, the kitchen prepares exactly

00:01:47.008 --> 00:01:49.618
that, and the waiter brings
it back to your table.

00:01:49.991 --> 00:01:52.091
That waiter is an API.

00:01:52.131 --> 00:01:56.011
API stands for Application Programming
Interface, but you can forget

00:01:56.051 --> 00:01:57.571
that phrase right now.

00:01:57.611 --> 00:02:01.751
Just remember: an API is the waiter
between you and somebody else's

00:02:01.791 --> 00:02:04.267
kitchen. Now let's swap in BrightLeaf.

00:02:04.307 --> 00:02:07.867
The kitchen is Odoo, the ERP
you set up in the last video.

00:02:07.907 --> 00:02:11.507
The customer is Stocky, the
AI agent we're going to build.

00:02:11.547 --> 00:02:14.127
Stocky never logs into
Odoo like Sarah does.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:15.907
It doesn't click menus.

00:02:15.947 --> 00:02:19.647
It sends an order to Odoo's waiter:
"Please bring me the on-hand

00:02:19.687 --> 00:02:24.467
quantity for SKU BL-1005,
the pallet jack wheels."

00:02:24.507 --> 00:02:27.237
And the waiter comes back
with: "Eighteen sets."

00:02:27.454 --> 00:02:29.334
Here's why companies love this.

00:02:29.374 --> 00:02:30.794
The kitchen stays safe.

00:02:30.834 --> 00:02:33.914
Odoo decides what the waiter
is allowed to bring out.

00:02:33.954 --> 00:02:37.554
Sarah's customer list stays private,
the accounting stays locked,

00:02:37.594 --> 00:02:39.679
and Stocky gets only what it needs.

00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:42.489
Three more words and you've
got the whole vocabulary.

00:02:42.806 --> 00:02:45.106
The order you give is called a request.

00:02:45.146 --> 00:02:47.226
What comes back to your
table is the response.

00:02:47.266 --> 00:02:50.367
Simple. And every restaurant has a menu.

00:02:50.407 --> 00:02:53.167
You can't order sushi in a pizza place.

00:02:53.207 --> 00:02:57.887
APIs are the same. Each API has a
list of things it can do, and each

00:02:57.927 --> 00:03:00.607
item on that list has its own address.

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:03.067
That address is called an endpoint.

00:03:03.107 --> 00:03:06.287
One endpoint might be
"today's weather forecast".

00:03:06.327 --> 00:03:08.987
Another might be "latest exchange rates".

00:03:09.027 --> 00:03:11.777
Think of an endpoint as
one dish on the menu.

00:03:11.979 --> 00:03:14.099
This is what a real order looks like.

00:03:14.139 --> 00:03:16.129
Don't panic, we'll take it apart.

00:03:16.341 --> 00:03:21.701
The blue part, api dot open-meteo
dot com, is the restaurant's address.

00:03:21.741 --> 00:03:23.451
Which building are we walking into?

00:03:23.729 --> 00:03:28.329
The green part, slash v1 slash
forecast, is the endpoint.

00:03:28.369 --> 00:03:29.389
Which dish do we want?

00:03:29.429 --> 00:03:33.825
The forecast. The orange part,
everything after the question mark,

00:03:33.865 --> 00:03:35.805
is our order details.

00:03:35.845 --> 00:03:41.405
Latitude 22.54 and
longitude 114.06, that's

00:03:41.445 --> 00:03:47.005
Shenzhen. Then which bits of the
forecast we want: rainfall and wind.

00:03:47.045 --> 00:03:50.865
The question mark means "here
come my order details", and each

00:03:50.905 --> 00:03:54.885
ampersand, that little "and"
sign, means "and also".

00:03:54.925 --> 00:03:58.865
It's exactly like saying, "One
chicken burrito, not too spicy,

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:00.055
and extra guacamole."

00:04:01.167 --> 00:04:04.893
Let's go back to Sarah and
see where this could help her.

00:04:04.933 --> 00:04:09.316
Last September, BrightLeaf ordered
60 sets of pallet jack wheels,

00:04:09.356 --> 00:04:13.117
BL-1005, from Global
Lift Parts in Shenzhen.

00:04:13.228 --> 00:04:17.176
They were $38 a set,
with a 21-day lead time.

00:04:17.216 --> 00:04:20.812
Tom's team in Columbus needed them
because three of their five pallet

00:04:20.852 --> 00:04:22.376
jacks had worn out wheels.

00:04:22.416 --> 00:04:25.229
On a Tuesday, a rainstorm
hit southern China.

00:04:25.269 --> 00:04:29.102
The port slowed down for three days,
the container missed its vessel,

00:04:29.142 --> 00:04:31.222
and the wheels arrived nine days late.

00:04:31.262 --> 00:04:34.982
A weather warning had been available
on Sunday, but Sarah did not hear

00:04:35.022 --> 00:04:38.246
about the delay until a supplier
emailed the following Monday.

00:04:38.286 --> 00:04:42.476
She was handling everything else,
and nobody was checking Shenzhen's

00:04:42.516 --> 00:04:43.636
weather every morning.

00:04:43.833 --> 00:04:47.999
For those 9 days, Tom's team worked
with two pallet jacks instead of

00:04:48.039 --> 00:04:51.989
five. That meant overtime, a late
customer shipment, and a difficult

00:04:52.199 --> 00:04:53.039
conversation with Mr.

00:04:53.079 --> 00:04:56.993
Harrison. Now imagine Stocky
checking a weather API each morning.

00:04:57.105 --> 00:05:02.125
On Sunday, it could flag heavy rain
expected in Shenzhen from Tuesday to

00:05:02.165 --> 00:05:05.950
Thursday and tell Sarah that two
open orders might be affected.

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:10.500
She could follow up with the supplier
before the disruption and discuss

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:14.353
options with Tom. The weather would
still happen, but she have time to

00:05:14.393 --> 00:05:18.040
prepare. Let's try that weather API
now so you can see the information

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:18.920
for yourself.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.660
Okay, this is the moment.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:26.260
I've opened a brand-new browser tab,
the same browser you use for email.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.780
I'm pasting in the web
address from the screen.

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:32.410
You'll find it in your activity file
too, so you don't have to type it.

00:05:32.727 --> 00:05:34.087
Notice what we're NOT doing.

00:05:34.127 --> 00:05:35.267
We're not signing up.

00:05:35.307 --> 00:05:37.367
We're not downloading anything.

00:05:37.407 --> 00:05:40.947
Open-Meteo is a free weather
service that lets anyone ask for a

00:05:40.987 --> 00:05:42.687
forecast. No key card needed.

00:05:42.727 --> 00:05:45.462
I press Enter. And there it is.

00:05:45.502 --> 00:05:47.622
The waiter is back at our table.

00:05:47.662 --> 00:05:51.422
This is a real response, from a
real weather service, about the real

00:05:51.462 --> 00:05:54.252
city of Shenzhen, and it
took less than a second.

00:05:54.504 --> 00:05:55.864
I know what you're thinking.

00:05:55.904 --> 00:05:58.344
"This looks like computer gibberish."

00:05:58.384 --> 00:05:59.764
Give me sixty seconds.

00:05:59.804 --> 00:06:01.494
It's actually a shopping list.

00:06:01.764 --> 00:06:03.964
This format is called JSON.

00:06:04.004 --> 00:06:05.984
Say it like the name "Jason".

00:06:06.024 --> 00:06:10.404
And the easiest way to think about
JSON is as a labelled shopping list.

00:06:10.444 --> 00:06:14.404
Every line has a label on the left,
in quotes, then a colon, then the

00:06:14.444 --> 00:06:15.474
value on the right.

00:06:15.688 --> 00:06:20.208
So here: label "latitude", value 22.53.

00:06:20.248 --> 00:06:24.308
Label "timezone", value "Asia/Shanghai".

00:06:24.348 --> 00:06:28.668
It's the same as a shopping
list that says "Milk: 2 litres.

00:06:28.708 --> 00:06:34.954
Eggs: 12." Notice the latitude
came back as 22.53, not exactly the

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:37.454
22.54 we asked for.

00:06:37.494 --> 00:06:41.094
That's the weather service saying,
"my nearest weather grid point is

00:06:41.134 --> 00:06:43.052
here." Close enough for a city.

00:06:43.352 --> 00:06:45.472
Now this bit is really helpful.

00:06:45.512 --> 00:06:49.372
"Daily units." It tells us rainfall
is measured in millimetres,

00:06:49.412 --> 00:06:52.092
and wind speed in kilometres per hour.

00:06:52.132 --> 00:06:55.072
A good API always tells you its units.

00:06:55.112 --> 00:06:59.292
Hold on to that idea, because
in the next video, on databases,

00:06:59.332 --> 00:07:02.422
mixed-up units are going
to be our enemy number one.

00:07:02.603 --> 00:07:04.623
Here's the actual forecast.

00:07:04.663 --> 00:07:08.515
Square brackets mean "a list of
several items", like a column in Excel.

00:07:08.895 --> 00:07:11.075
"Time" is a list of seven dates.

00:07:11.115 --> 00:07:15.455
On the day I recorded this, it
started with the 22nd of November and

00:07:15.495 --> 00:07:19.737
went to the 28th. "Precipitation
sum" is a list of seven rainfall

00:07:19.777 --> 00:07:25.067
numbers: 0, 0.1, then
48.4, then mostly zeros.

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:31.991
"Wind speed max" is seven wind
numbers: 16.8, 20.9, 20.7, and

00:07:32.031 --> 00:07:35.918
so on. The trick is to read
them like columns that line up.

00:07:35.958 --> 00:07:39.438
The first date goes with the first
rain number and the first wind

00:07:39.478 --> 00:07:45.338
number. So the 22nd: no rain, wind
up to 16.8 kilometres an hour.

00:07:45.378 --> 00:07:49.938
But look at the 24th:
48.4 millimetres of rain.

00:07:49.978 --> 00:07:53.918
That's a seriously wet day, exactly
the kind of number that should make

00:07:53.958 --> 00:07:57.998
Sarah call Global Lift Parts and ask
whether her pallet jack wheels are

00:07:58.038 --> 00:08:02.818
still on schedule. And if the wind
went above 60 or 70, you'd also want

00:08:02.858 --> 00:08:04.748
to ask whether the port is running.

00:08:05.009 --> 00:08:08.009
Now watch how easy it
is to change the order.

00:08:08.049 --> 00:08:14.269
Gulf Lubricants, who supply our
hydraulic oil, BL-1007, are in Dubai.

00:08:14.309 --> 00:08:20.249
Dubai is latitude 25.20, longitude 55.27.

00:08:20.289 --> 00:08:23.319
I change just those two
numbers and press Enter.

00:08:23.595 --> 00:08:28.555
New response. The timezone now says
"Asia/Dubai", and the rainfall is

00:08:28.595 --> 00:08:31.055
mostly zeros. No surprise there.

00:08:31.095 --> 00:08:34.275
I changed the order details, and
the waiter brought back a different

00:08:34.315 --> 00:08:38.635
dish. That is literally all Stocky
does when it checks five supplier

00:08:38.675 --> 00:08:42.665
cities. Same request, five
different sets of order details.

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:45.940
Let's try a second restaurant.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:49.420
This one is called Frankfurter,
and it serves exchange rates.

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:51.890
It's free and needs no key card either.

00:08:52.187 --> 00:08:53.687
Read the order with me.

00:08:53.727 --> 00:08:57.627
The endpoint is "latest",
meaning the latest rates.

00:08:57.667 --> 00:09:03.507
The order details: "from" equals
USD, the US dollar, and "to" equals

00:09:03.547 --> 00:09:09.627
CNY, MXN and AED. Chinese
yuan, Mexican peso and UAE

00:09:09.667 --> 00:09:14.427
dirham, the three foreign currencies
BrightLeaf's suppliers bill in.

00:09:14.467 --> 00:09:17.218
Enter. First, a little surprise.

00:09:17.258 --> 00:09:18.898
Look at the address bar.

00:09:18.938 --> 00:09:20.538
It changed by itself.

00:09:20.578 --> 00:09:23.918
We typed frankfurter dot app, and
now it says frankfurter dot dev,

00:09:23.958 --> 00:09:29.918
slash v1. The restaurant moved to a
new building and left a forwarding

00:09:29.958 --> 00:09:31.498
note on the old door.

00:09:31.538 --> 00:09:35.058
Your browser read the note
and walked over automatically.

00:09:35.098 --> 00:09:38.928
APIs do move house sometimes, and
good ones leave a forwarding address.

00:09:39.311 --> 00:09:41.051
Now the shopping list.

00:09:41.091 --> 00:09:43.931
"Amount": 1. "Base": USD.

00:09:43.971 --> 00:09:46.211
"Date": the 20th of November.

00:09:46.251 --> 00:09:51.951
Then "rates", with a smaller
list inside it: CNY 6.68, MXN

00:09:51.991 --> 00:09:56.971
19.07. A list inside a list, like a
shopping list with a "Dairy" section

00:09:57.011 --> 00:09:58.461
that has its own items.

00:09:58.695 --> 00:10:04.715
So on test day, one dollar
bought 6.68 yuan and 19.07 pesos.

00:10:04.755 --> 00:10:07.235
Your numbers will be different,
and that's the point.

00:10:07.275 --> 00:10:10.477
It's live. Let's make that real for Sarah.

00:10:10.517 --> 00:10:12.917
Global Lift Parts bills in yuan.

00:10:12.957 --> 00:10:17.500
Sixty sets of pallet jack wheels at
thirty-eight dollars is two thousand

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:22.317
two hundred and eighty dollars,
which at 6.68 is an invoice of about

00:10:22.357 --> 00:10:24.657
fifteen thousand two hundred yuan.

00:10:24.697 --> 00:10:28.757
Now say the yuan gets three percent
stronger, and a dollar only buys

00:10:28.797 --> 00:10:35.206
about 6.48 yuan. That same yuan
invoice now costs about two thousand

00:10:35.246 --> 00:10:37.028
three hundred and fifty dollars.

00:10:37.068 --> 00:10:41.237
Sixty-eight dollars more, without
anyone changing the price.

00:10:41.277 --> 00:10:44.497
Same story for SafeHands gloves in pesos.

00:10:44.537 --> 00:10:47.047
Stocky can spot that
the moment it happens.

00:10:47.414 --> 00:10:49.694
Now, did you notice something missing?

00:10:49.734 --> 00:10:51.274
We asked for three currencies.

00:10:51.314 --> 00:10:53.382
We got two. Where's the dirham?

00:10:53.822 --> 00:10:57.622
Great lesson. Frankfurter gets its
numbers from the European Central

00:10:57.662 --> 00:11:00.782
Bank, and that kitchen
doesn't stock the dirham.

00:11:00.822 --> 00:11:02.902
The waiter didn't crash or argue.

00:11:02.942 --> 00:11:04.752
It just brought what the kitchen had.

00:11:05.146 --> 00:11:09.666
Luckily the dirham is pegged to the
dollar at about 3.67, so it barely

00:11:09.706 --> 00:11:14.346
moves. But the habit matters: always
check that what came back matches

00:11:14.386 --> 00:11:15.826
what you asked for.

00:11:15.866 --> 00:11:18.476
We'll teach Stocky to
notice missing items too.

00:11:19.117 --> 00:11:23.614
So far, we have asked for information:
a weather forecast and some

00:11:23.654 --> 00:11:26.676
exchange rates. We haven't
changed anything in those systems.

00:11:26.716 --> 00:11:29.083
Those browser calls are
called GET requests.

00:11:29.123 --> 00:11:33.030
Think of GET as asking the waiter
to bring you some information.

00:11:33.233 --> 00:11:35.226
POST is used to submit something.

00:11:35.266 --> 00:11:38.793
In our restaurant example, that's
handing over an order slip for the

00:11:38.833 --> 00:11:44.148
kitchen to act on. At BrightLeaf,
checking how many boxes of BL-1002 are

00:11:44.188 --> 00:11:45.928
in stock is reading information.

00:11:45.968 --> 00:11:50.924
Creating a purchase order for 1820
boxes from PackRight Industries is

00:11:50.964 --> 00:11:54.014
taking an action. A new
record would exist in Odoo.

00:11:54.128 --> 00:11:58.094
There are other methods for updates
and deletions, and the exact method

00:11:58.134 --> 00:11:59.313
depends upon the API.

00:11:59.353 --> 00:12:03.530
The distinction to remember is
reading information versus submitting

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:05.001
something that could change it.

00:12:05.041 --> 00:12:07.312
That matters when we give Stocky tools.

00:12:07.352 --> 00:12:11.368
It can check information on its own,
but important changes need Sarah's

00:12:11.408 --> 00:12:14.246
approval first. Let's
practise with four tasks.

00:12:14.286 --> 00:12:18.796
For each one, decide whether we are
reading information or submitting

00:12:18.866 --> 00:12:22.264
something. First, checking
a supplier's lead time.

00:12:22.304 --> 00:12:26.481
Second, updating the reorder
point for BL-1004 to 700.

00:12:26.521 --> 00:12:30.232
Third, sending a delivery
reminder email to SafeHands Co.

00:12:30.272 --> 00:12:33.442
And fourth, reading this
week's sales orders.

00:12:33.482 --> 00:12:36.319
Pause here and think through your answers.

00:12:44.067 --> 00:12:48.268
Alright. Checking the lead time and
reading sales orders are requests

00:12:48.308 --> 00:12:50.804
for information, like our GET examples.

00:12:50.967 --> 00:12:53.906
Changing the reorder point and
sending the email are actions.

00:12:53.946 --> 00:12:57.347
They belong on the submitting side,
where we need to think about the

00:12:57.387 --> 00:12:58.825
permission and approval.

00:12:58.865 --> 00:13:02.065
Keep that distinction in mind
when we start building Stocky.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:06.240
Every time the waiter comes back,
he also hands you a little receipt

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:07.780
with a number on it.

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:11.040
This number is called a status
code, and it tells you how the order

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:13.037
went, before you even look at the plate.

00:13:13.297 --> 00:13:15.197
You don't need to memorise hundreds.

00:13:15.237 --> 00:13:17.527
Four will cover almost
everything you'll meet.

00:13:17.997 --> 00:13:21.817
200, OK. Your order went
perfectly, here's your food.

00:13:21.857 --> 00:13:24.327
Both our calls today came back with a 200.

00:13:25.153 --> 00:13:27.633
401, wrong key card.

00:13:27.673 --> 00:13:30.793
You tried to open a door
your card doesn't open.

00:13:30.833 --> 00:13:34.113
The API is saying, "I
don't know who you are."

00:13:34.153 --> 00:13:37.983
Usually it means your API key is
missing, mistyped or cancelled.

00:13:38.828 --> 00:13:40.668
404, wrong address.

00:13:40.708 --> 00:13:44.908
Not Found. You asked for a dish that
isn't on the menu, or you walked to

00:13:44.948 --> 00:13:49.158
the wrong table. Nine times out of
ten, it's a typo in the web address.

00:13:50.217 --> 00:13:52.137
429, too many orders.

00:13:52.177 --> 00:13:54.097
You're ordering too fast.

00:13:54.137 --> 00:13:58.257
Free APIs often say, "Please slow
down, you've asked me a thousand times

00:13:58.297 --> 00:14:00.677
this minute." The fix is simple.

00:14:00.717 --> 00:14:03.313
Wait and try again, or ask less often.

00:14:03.613 --> 00:14:07.353
Here's a shortcut. Anything
starting with a 2 is good news.

00:14:07.393 --> 00:14:10.993
Anything starting with a 4 means
something was wrong with your order.

00:14:11.033 --> 00:14:15.333
Anything starting with a 5, like
500, means the kitchen itself had a

00:14:15.373 --> 00:14:17.453
problem, and it's not your fault.

00:14:17.493 --> 00:14:19.323
Wait a few minutes and try again.

00:14:19.567 --> 00:14:21.547
Now let's break something on purpose.

00:14:21.587 --> 00:14:23.637
You can't break anything here, I promise.

00:14:23.822 --> 00:14:25.722
I'm going to misspell "forecast".

00:14:25.762 --> 00:14:27.302
I'll take out the "a".

00:14:27.342 --> 00:14:29.164
Enter. Look at that.

00:14:29.204 --> 00:14:30.684
"Reason: Not Found.

00:14:30.724 --> 00:14:34.444
Error: true." That's our 404 receipt.

00:14:34.484 --> 00:14:38.144
We asked for a dish called "forecst",
and there's no such dish on the

00:14:38.184 --> 00:14:40.464
menu. One missing letter.

00:14:40.504 --> 00:14:44.364
This is the single most common
beginner error, and now you've seen it.

00:14:44.404 --> 00:14:45.914
You'll recognise it forever.

00:14:46.182 --> 00:14:48.682
Let's fix that and try
a different mistake.

00:14:48.722 --> 00:14:52.622
This time the dish is right, but
I'll ask for a silly order detail.

00:14:52.662 --> 00:14:54.992
Instead of rainfall,
I'll ask for "banana".

00:14:55.219 --> 00:14:58.839
The restaurant exists, the dish
exists, but the order doesn't make

00:14:58.879 --> 00:15:02.719
sense. The waiter says, "I
can't give you a daily banana."

00:15:02.759 --> 00:15:07.999
That's a 400, "bad request", another
4-something, so it's on our side.

00:15:08.039 --> 00:15:09.559
And look how helpful it is.

00:15:09.599 --> 00:15:12.559
It tells us exactly which
word it didn't understand.

00:15:12.599 --> 00:15:14.959
When something goes
wrong, read the message.

00:15:14.999 --> 00:15:16.809
It usually tells you what to fix.

00:15:16.963 --> 00:15:18.943
And we're back to a 200.

00:15:18.983 --> 00:15:20.633
Everything fixed in two seconds.

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:24.840
The weather and currency
APIs serve anyone.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:28.560
But Odoo holds BrightLeaf's
stock, costs and customers.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:31.390
You don't want the whole world
asking that waiter for things.

00:15:31.637 --> 00:15:36.617
So private APIs ask for an API key,
and an API key is like a hotel room

00:15:36.657 --> 00:15:40.137
key card. It opens your
room, maybe the gym.

00:15:40.177 --> 00:15:43.497
Not other guests' rooms,
not the manager's office.

00:15:43.537 --> 00:15:47.137
Lose it, and reception cancels
it and gives you a new one.

00:15:47.177 --> 00:15:48.837
Your passport doesn't change.

00:15:48.877 --> 00:15:52.004
Just the card. An API
key works the same way.

00:15:52.044 --> 00:15:56.164
It opens only what it's allowed
to, it belongs to a specific user,

00:15:56.204 --> 00:15:59.334
and it can be cancelled any time
without changing your password.

00:15:59.627 --> 00:16:02.287
Let me show you where Odoo
keeps these key cards.

00:16:02.327 --> 00:16:05.947
We won't create one today; we'll
do that when we connect Stocky.

00:16:05.987 --> 00:16:08.157
I just want you to know
where reception is.

00:16:08.346 --> 00:16:12.186
In the BrightLeaf Odoo trial, I open
the Settings app and scroll to the

00:16:12.226 --> 00:16:16.246
bottom. There's a link called
"Activate the developer mode".

00:16:16.286 --> 00:16:17.806
Don't be put off by the name.

00:16:17.846 --> 00:16:20.226
It just shows a few extra options.

00:16:20.266 --> 00:16:21.956
Click it, and the page reloads.

00:16:22.326 --> 00:16:25.786
Now I click my profile picture
in the top right corner, then "My

00:16:25.826 --> 00:16:29.386
Preferences". In some versions
it's called "My Profile".

00:16:29.426 --> 00:16:31.236
Then the tab called "Security".

00:16:31.552 --> 00:16:36.892
And here it is. "API Keys", with
a button called "Create API Key".

00:16:36.932 --> 00:16:40.732
When we create one later, Odoo
will show the key exactly once,

00:16:40.772 --> 00:16:44.732
a long string of letters and numbers,
and we'll paste it straight into a

00:16:44.772 --> 00:16:47.702
safe place. Odoo never shows it again.

00:16:48.092 --> 00:16:49.552
Notice something important.

00:16:49.592 --> 00:16:51.592
This key belongs to a user.

00:16:51.632 --> 00:16:55.932
When Stocky uses Sarah's key, Odoo
treats Stocky like Sarah, with the

00:16:55.972 --> 00:17:00.532
same permissions. That's why, when
we go live, we'll give Stocky its own

00:17:00.572 --> 00:17:03.012
user with only the access it needs.

00:17:03.052 --> 00:17:06.322
Its own key card, not
the manager's master key.

00:17:06.656 --> 00:17:08.416
Three key card rules.

00:17:08.456 --> 00:17:12.816
One: never paste a key into an
email, a WhatsApp chat or a shared

00:17:12.856 --> 00:17:17.876
document. Two: give each tool its
own key, so cancelling one doesn't

00:17:17.916 --> 00:17:22.656
break the others. Three: if a key
might have leaked, delete it here and

00:17:22.696 --> 00:17:26.166
make a new one. Ten seconds,
and nothing else breaks.

00:17:26.933 --> 00:17:30.573
Before we finish, there are four
things I'd like you to check whenever

00:17:30.613 --> 00:17:33.337
you use an API. First, check the address.

00:17:33.377 --> 00:17:36.909
We saw how one missing
letter gave us a 404.

00:17:36.949 --> 00:17:41.074
A space, a missing character or the
wrong separator can stop a request

00:17:41.114 --> 00:17:44.460
from working. Read the address
carefully before trying again.

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:46.177
Second, check the units.

00:17:46.217 --> 00:17:49.209
Our weather response used
millimetres for rainfall.

00:17:49.249 --> 00:17:51.234
Another API might use inches.

00:17:51.274 --> 00:17:54.594
The number only makes sense when
you know what it is measuring.

00:17:54.733 --> 00:17:58.663
Third, check that you have received
everything you have asked for.

00:17:58.722 --> 00:18:02.357
We asked for three currencies, but
the dirham was not in the response.

00:18:02.397 --> 00:18:06.224
Getting an answer doesn't
automatically mean every item is there.

00:18:06.264 --> 00:18:08.821
That's something we will
want Stocky to check too.

00:18:09.033 --> 00:18:11.398
Fourth, be careful with API keys.

00:18:11.438 --> 00:18:14.744
Keep them out of team
chats and shared documents.

00:18:14.784 --> 00:18:18.173
Remember, a key can give someone
access to company information.

00:18:18.213 --> 00:18:21.717
If it may have been exposed,
cancel it and create a new one.

00:18:22.033 --> 00:18:25.953
We have now made two API calls, read
the responses, and worked through

00:18:25.993 --> 00:18:30.062
errors on purpose. When we connect
Stocky later, you will be able to

00:18:30.102 --> 00:18:34.008
recognise those same requests,
responses and errors in the workflow.

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:38.611
All right, you have now used two APIs
yourself and seen what comes back

00:18:38.651 --> 00:18:40.804
when a request works or goes wrong.

00:18:40.844 --> 00:18:43.284
Let's go over the three
main things to remember.

00:18:43.467 --> 00:18:47.495
First, think of an API as a waiter
between Stocky and another system.

00:18:47.535 --> 00:18:52.176
Stocky sends a request to an endpoint,
includes the details it needs as

00:18:52.216 --> 00:18:54.386
parameters, and gets a response back.

00:18:54.567 --> 00:18:57.355
Second, the response often comes as JSON.

00:18:57.395 --> 00:19:01.169
Think of the labelled shopping list:
a label and its value, with lists

00:19:01.209 --> 00:19:04.258
you can read alongside each
other like Excel columns.

00:19:04.298 --> 00:19:06.883
The status code tells
you how the request went.

00:19:06.923 --> 00:19:11.657
200 means OK. 401 means
an authentication problem.

00:19:11.697 --> 00:19:13.373
404 means not found.

00:19:13.413 --> 00:19:16.255
429 means you are asking too often.

00:19:16.433 --> 00:19:19.713
And third, remember the difference
between reading information and

00:19:19.753 --> 00:19:23.707
taking action. GET and POST
introduced us to that distinction.

00:19:23.747 --> 00:19:28.258
Important changes need Sarah's
approval, and private systems like Odoo

00:19:28.298 --> 00:19:30.498
need an API key that controls access.

00:19:30.574 --> 00:19:35.150
Your activity is the workbook called
"V03 Activity, API Treasure Hunt".

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:39.493
You will try weather, currency
and Mexico public holiday APIs,

00:19:39.533 --> 00:19:41.093
then record what you find.

00:19:41.133 --> 00:19:44.952
We will also turn a stormy-week
weather response into a table,

00:19:44.992 --> 00:19:49.648
match status codes to their meanings,
and practise sorting GET and POST

00:19:49.688 --> 00:19:53.596
tasks. The last tab builds weather
and currency links for your own

00:19:53.706 --> 00:19:57.339
suppliers. Allow about 30 minutes
for the activity, then take the

00:19:57.379 --> 00:20:02.372
ten-question quiz. In video 4,
"Databases Without Fear: Your Data's

00:20:02.412 --> 00:20:05.939
Home", we will look at where Stocky
stores what it found and why it made

00:20:05.979 --> 00:20:10.147
a decision. We will use Google
Sheets as its memory and clean up a

00:20:10.187 --> 00:20:14.577
BrightLeaf export, including merged
cells, duplicates and values like

00:20:14.617 --> 00:20:16.853
"approx 300". I'll see you there.
